@node	0.7, 0.8, Chapter 0, Chapter 0
@section Closures and lexical scope

@noindent
A function defined inside another captures the bindings visible where it is
defined, not where it is later called, and keeps them alive for as long as
the function itself exists. This is a @newterm{closure}:

@example
# let make_adder n = fun x -> x + n;;
val make_adder : int -> int -> int = <fun>
# let add_five = make_adder 5;;
val add_five : int -> int = <fun>
# add_five 1;;
- : int = 6
@end example

@noindent
@code{make_adder 5} returns a function that still remembers @code{n = 5}
long after @code{make_adder} itself has returned; @code{add_five} is that
function, and every call to it sees the same captured @code{5}. Nothing
about @code{n} is passed to @code{add_five} at call time: it is baked into
the function value itself.

@subsubheading Functions as values

Because functions are ordinary values, one function can take another as an
argument or return one as a result with no special syntax beyond what
already defines a function:

@example
# let compose f g = fun x -> f (g x);;
val compose : ('a -> 'b) -> ('c -> 'a) -> 'c -> 'b = <fun>
# let doubled_and_shifted = compose (fun x -> x * 2) (fun x -> x + 3);;
val doubled_and_shifted : int -> int = <fun>
# doubled_and_shifted 4;;
- : int = 14
@end example

@noindent
@code{compose f g} applies @code{g} first and @code{f} second, so
@code{doubled_and_shifted} adds three, then doubles the result: @code{(4 +
3) * 2 = 14}. No object system, method table, or special ``function type''
declaration was needed to make this work; @code{'a -> 'b} already is a type,
and functions of that type are values like any other.

@subsubheading What function equality does not have, and closures do have

Two closures built from the same source expression are never
@code{==}-identical if each captured its own fresh state, because each call
to the outer function that builds them allocates a fresh cell for whatever
it captures. There is, in fact, no way to compare two functions for equality
at all in OCaml: @code{=} on a function type is a runtime error, not a
sound implementation of ``do these functions compute the same thing''
(@code{Stdlib} raises @code{Invalid_argument} rather than approximate an
answer no general algorithm can give). What a closure @emph{does} have,
reliably, is the identity of whatever mutable cell it captured, exactly the
@code{==} from section 0.6 applied to that cell:

@example
# let make_withdraw initial =
    let balance = ref initial in
    fun amount ->
      if !balance < amount
      then None
      else (
        balance := !balance - amount;
        Some !balance);;
val make_withdraw : int -> int -> int option = <fun>
# let w = make_withdraw 100;;
val w : int -> int option = <fun>
# w 60;;
- : int option = Some 40
# w 60;;
- : int option = None
@end example

@noindent
This is the same @code{make_withdraw} example the original book builds in
its chapter 3: each
call to @code{make_withdraw} allocates its own private @code{balance} cell,
and the returned function is a closure over that one cell. The first
withdrawal of @code{60} succeeds, leaving @code{40}; the second, from the
same @code{w}, fails, because @code{40 < 60}, and the failed attempt leaves the cell untouched: @code{None} carries no balance, by design, so a failed
withdrawal cannot be mistaken for a successful one that happened to return
zero.

@subsubheading Independent private state, one closure at a time

Two calls to @code{make_withdraw} allocate two distinct cells, so the two
withdrawers returned never interfere with each other, exactly the way two
calls to @code{fresh_counter} in section 0.6 would build two independent
counters:

@example
# let w1 = make_withdraw 100;;
val w1 : int -> int option = <fun>
# let w2 = make_withdraw 100;;
val w2 : int -> int option = <fun>
# w1 20;;
- : int option = Some 80
# w2 30;;
- : int option = Some 70
# w1 80;;
- : int option = Some 0
@end example

@noindent
@code{w1} and @code{w2} both started at @code{100}, but withdrawing through
@code{w2} never touches @code{w1}'s balance: @code{w1}'s final withdrawal of
@code{80} finds the balance still at @code{80}, unaffected by @code{w2}'s
withdrawal of @code{30} in between. This is the object-oriented idea chapter
3 develops formally: an object as a bundle of private mutable state plus the operations that touch it. It is built here from nothing but closures and @code{ref}. A record whose fields are themselves closures over one shared
cell is the next step, combining this section with section 0.6's records:
exercise 0.4's @code{make_account} bundles @code{deposit}, @code{withdraw},
and @code{balance} into one such record, so a single account value offers
all three as one unit.

@subsubheading Exercise

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.4}Exercise 0.4:} Build @code{make_account}, which
returns a record of three closures over one @code{ref} of private balance:
@code{deposit} an amount and get the new balance, @code{withdraw} an amount
and get the new balance, and @code{balance} read it with no argument at all.
Then break the abstraction on purpose with @code{make_shared_accounts}: build
two accounts over a single shared @code{ref}, deposit through one, observe
through the other, and explain in a sentence what you see and why.
@end quotation

